  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 225 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nBehind the cold front, a seasonably cool and dry airmass (850-hPa \ntemperatures and PW below 25th climo percentile) will infiltrate the \nMid-Mississippi River Valley through Thursday night as a surface \nanticyclone extends into the region. As result, dry conditions, high \ntemperatures in the low to mid-80s F, and low temperatures in the \nupper 50s to mid-60s F are forecast through Friday night before the \nanticyclone starts retreating and low-level flow becomes southerly \nin response.\n\nThe anticyclone departure will be in part due to a broad, upper-\nlevel ridge amplifying and shifting eastward across the Mississippi \nRiver Valley over the weekend. Strong low-level southerly WAA will \nquickly warm 850-hPa temperatures to above the 95th climatological \npercentile by late Sunday with high temperatures reaching the 90s F \nacross most of the CWA into early next week. Moisture will be slower \nto increase, but global model guidance indicates a connection to the \nGulf of Mexico will finally be established around Monday , leading \nto rising heat index values to around 100 F in the warmest \nlocations. Monday, onward, model guidance is in disagreement on the \nposition and presence of smaller scale troughs and ridges embedded \nin approximately zonal upper-level flow, but there is an underlying \nsignal for slowly falling heights. This evolution introduces the \nconcerns of a frontal boundary to shift closer to at least \nnortheastern MO and west-central IL at some point with increasing \npotential for showers and thunderstorms to disrupt the longevity of \nheat in that part of the CWA. However, the details of these types of \npatterns are impossible to ascertain at this timerange.",
